WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.029
Storage is where upload mistakes become
persistent.

2
00:00:03.109 --> 00:00:06.425
Temporary upload locations should be
isolated, access-controlled,

3
00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:08.865
and cleaned up reliably after success or
failure.

4
00:00:08.945 --> 00:00:12.317
Permanent storage should avoid executable
application directories,

5
00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:15.712
classpath locations, deployment
directories, source directories,

6
00:00:15.792 --> 00:00:19.333
and public web roots unless public hosting
is an intentional and carefully

7
00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:20.368
controlled design.

8
00:00:20.448 --> 00:00:24.438
An uploaded file should not become
executable or directly reachable just because it

9
00:00:24.518 --> 00:00:26.148
was stored in a convenient folder.

10
00:00:26.228 --> 00:00:29.487
Use generated names and maintain metadata
in a database when needed.

11
00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:31.028
If filesystem storage is used,

12
00:00:31.108 --> 00:00:35.042
construct paths from an
application-controlled base and generated identifiers,

13
00:00:35.122 --> 00:00:36.077
not from user input.

14
00:00:36.157 --> 00:00:39.080
Java NIO path handling can help, but it
must be used carefully:

15
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.745
resolve the candidate path against the
intended base,

16
00:00:41.825 --> 00:00:44.578
normalize or canonicalize as appropriate
for the design,

17
00:00:44.658 --> 00:00:48.648
and confirm the result remains inside the
allowed storage boundary before writing,

18
00:00:48.728 --> 00:00:50.808
reading, deleting, or processing the file.

19
00:00:50.888 --> 00:00:54.653
Avoid using untrusted input to construct
filesystem paths, URLs, object keys,

20
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:58.442
shell commands, archive extraction
destinations, or processing locations.

21
00:00:58.522 --> 00:01:01.950
Object storage does not eliminate
path-like thinking; keys, prefixes,

22
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.964
bucket policies, signed URLs, lifecycle
rules, and access controls still matter.

23
00:01:06.044 --> 00:01:08.123
A tenant identifier, object key, filename,

24
00:01:08.203 --> 00:01:12.081
or callback URL taken from the client
should be validated and authorized before it

25
00:01:12.161 --> 00:01:14.240
influences storage or retrieval behavior.

26
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.254
Files should usually be private by default
and served through controlled download

27
00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:21.818
routes or time-limited signed URLs when
that matches the business model.

28
00:01:21.898 --> 00:01:25.157
Use appropriate filesystem permissions,
object storage policies,

29
00:01:25.237 --> 00:01:28.890
lifecycle rules, retention controls,
deletion behavior, and cleanup jobs.

30
00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.567
Retention and deletion are part of
security because old uploads can contain

31
00:01:32.647 --> 00:01:35.963
sensitive data, stale malware findings,
abandoned temporary files,

32
00:01:36.043 --> 00:01:38.740
or material that no longer has a business
reason to exist.
